# Ask a promptable segmenter (kind: "segmenter", e.g. SAM 2.1 hiera-small)
# which pixels of the CURRENT wrist frame belong to the object in the jaws.
#
# Called by the HAL's vision attachment-evidence producer
# (openral_hal._vision_attachment_evidence.VisionAttachmentEvidenceProducer) at
# attach / detach / regrasp events — one shot per event, NOT per frame. The
# perception node (openral_perception_ros) captures the latest frame of the
# requested camera, projects the prompt point through that camera's manifest
# intrinsics, runs the segmenter, and returns the binary mask. The producer then
# intersects the mask with the same frame's depth channel and fits the bounded
# primitives of an AttachedCollisionObject.
#
# The prompt is GEOMETRIC, never semantic: unlike LocateInView (which takes a
# free-text query and returns labelled, scored boxes), this takes a 3-D point
# and returns "which pixels belong to the thing under it". The segmenter never
# classifies, and — see mask_score_advisory below — the caller never gates on
# the model's confidence.
#
# Request:
#   stamp            — the attach instant the caller is asking about. The CALLER
#                      owns it, not the server: if the server stamped the reply
#                      the mask could be computed against a frame captured after
#                      the object had already moved in the jaws, and the trace
#                      would no longer replay (CLAUDE.md §1.8).
#   camera           — camera selector. Empty = the perception node's
#                      primary/default camera; otherwise one of its configured
#                      camera ids. NOT a hardcoded name.
#   frame_id         — tf2 frame that tcp_point / negative_points are expressed
#                      in (e.g. the gripper's attach link). The server transforms
#                      into the camera optical frame itself; TF2 stays the only
#                      source of coordinate frames (CLAUDE.md §2).
#   tcp_point        — the positive prompt: the Tool Center Point, in 3-D, in
#                      frame_id. Sent as a POINT rather than a pixel so the
#                      camera intrinsics stay entirely on the perception side of
#                      this boundary instead of leaking into the torch-free HAL.
#   negative_points  — optional negative prompts in the same frame (typically the
#                      jaw tips), used to push the mask off the gripper fingers.
#                      Bounded by the manifest's segmenter.max_prompt_points.
#
# Response:
#   ok                   — true iff a frame was available and the segmenter
#                          returned a mask that cleared the manifest's
#                          segmenter.min_mask_area_px. False otherwise; `mask` is
#                          then empty and the caller MUST fall back conservatively
#                          (jaw-span box, evidence_kind GRIPPER_FORCE) rather than
#                          leave the payload invisible to collision checking.
#   camera               — the camera id actually queried (echoes the resolved
#                          default).
#   mask                 — the binary mask as sensor_msgs/Image, encoding
#                          "mono8", at the source frame's own resolution: 255
#                          where the pixel belongs to the prompted object, 0
#                          elsewhere.
#
#                          RAW mono8, deliberately NOT run-length encoded. This
#                          is a one-shot event call (attach / detach / regrasp),
#                          not a 30 Hz stream, so a 640x480 mask is a single
#                          ~307 kB request — well inside the ~100 ms
#                          deferred-ack barrier the HAL already holds for depth.
#                          An RLE codec would buy a few hundred bytes in exchange
#                          for a bespoke encoder/decoder pair that both the
#                          perception node and the safety-adjacent HAL producer
#                          would have to agree on exactly, forever. sensor_msgs/
#                          Image is already normative, already introspectable
#                          with `ros2 topic echo`, and needs no codec in the
#                          safety-adjacent path.
#   mask_score_advisory  — the model's own IoU/confidence for the returned mask.
#                          ADVISORY ONLY, and named to say so. It is recorded in
#                          the trace and MUST NOT be thresholded: a mis-aimed
#                          point prompt was measured returning a 59.8%-of-frame
#                          mask (essentially the whole tablecloth) at this
#                          model's TOP score of 0.977. Every fail-closed gate on
#                          the caller's side is geometric — containment between
#                          the jaws, payload extent, depth validity — never this.
#   failure_reason       — typed string ('ROSPerceptionStale: …', empty on ok).

builtin_interfaces/Time stamp
string camera
string frame_id
geometry_msgs/Point tcp_point
geometry_msgs/Point[] negative_points
---
bool ok
string camera
sensor_msgs/Image mask
float32 mask_score_advisory
string failure_reason
